    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "I added a summary panel to the screen, and `bin/e2e` passes all 4 tests in the spec.\n\nThe panel works entirely in the browser from data the screen already loads, so the server and stored data didn't need to change. It shows:\n- **Per grade (A, B, C):** how many people are on that grade today, leaving out retired people, and their due-totals added up. Due-totals of \"n/a\" are skipped, the same way the existing totals skip them.\n- **Per period:** its due-total, the same figure as its row in the Periods list. That includes the frozen total of a closed period.\n- **Per status (DRAFT, OPEN, PENDING, SETTLED, VOID):** how many of the sheet's claims have that status. PENDING is counted because the test expects it, but nothing in the app sets that status today, so it always shows 0.\n\nClaims belonging to retired people still count in the status figures. The first test requires this, and it matches how the app already treats them: they're hidden from the list but count in every total.\n\n**How the figures stay in line with the list:** rather than copying the calculations, I moved the person and period due-total logic into two shared functions. The People and Periods lists now use the same code as the summary, so the numbers can't drift apart:\n- `useDueTotalOf` in `features/people/People.tsx`\n- `usePeriodDueTotal` in `features/periods/Periods.tsx`\n\nThe panel itself is in `features/summary/Summary.tsx` and is added after People in `routes/index.tsx`. The only other change is that the grade list in `People.tsx` is now exported so the summary can use it.",
